# Line-ending policy.
#
# core.autocrlf is true on the development machine, so without a rule here git
# rewrites every text file to CRLF on checkout. That is harmless for the C, INI,
# Python and Markdown sources, and it is deliberately left alone for them.
#
# It is NOT safe to depend on for shell scripts. `bash` treats a trailing \r as
# part of the token, so a CRLF script can fail with
#   set: pipefail\r: invalid option name
# depending on the bash build. Measured 2026-09-12: the Git Bash on this machine
# tolerates it, so `.githooks/pre-commit` is not broken today — this pin removes
# the dependency on that tolerance rather than fixing a live fault. The hooks are
# the one place it matters, because a hook that fails to parse fails OPEN: the
# commit proceeds unchecked.
#
# Scope is deliberately narrow. The only tracked shell script in the repo is
# .githooks/pre-commit, so this changes exactly one file's checkout form.

*.sh          text eol=lf
.githooks/*   text eol=lf
